[[File:Yonezawa Industrial Zone.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Yonezawa Industrial Zone, one of the largest manufacturing complexes in the Okami metropolitan region.]]

'''Yonezawa Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|米澤県|Yonezawa-ken}}) is a [[prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the central region of [[Harujima]] island. A [[landlocked country|landlocked]] prefecture, it is bordered by [[Okami Metropolis]] to the northeast, [[Yamagata Prefecture]] to the east, [[Tokuyama Prefecture]] to the south, and [[Kofu Prefecture]] to the west. Yonezawa serves as the primary '''industrial hinterland''' for the national capital, hosting extensive manufacturing zones, logistics centers, and government satellite offices that support the metropolitan region.

The capital and largest city is [[Yonezawa]], with a population of approximately 849,055 residents. The prefecture has a total population of 2,090,036 (2026), making it one of the most densely populated prefectures in Okaiken. Yonezawa Prefecture is renowned for its '''heavy manufacturing sector''', particularly automotive production, electronics assembly, and machinery manufacturing, as well as its role as a '''logistics hub''' connecting the capital to the rest of Harujima island.

==History==
{{Main|History of Yonezawa Prefecture}}

===Ancient Period and the Rice Granary===

[[File:Yonezawa Rice Terraces.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Historic rice terraces in the Nagai Valley, preserved from the Heiji period.]]

The Yonezawa region has been inhabited since the [[Jōmon period]], with archaeological evidence indicating settled agricultural communities as early as 300 BCE. The inland basin geography, ringed by protective hills, created a fertile microclimate ideal for rice cultivation. During the [[Asama period]] (710–794 AD), the region was known as the "Rice Granary" ({{nihongo|米倉|Yonegura}}), supplying tribute rice to the imperial court at [[Heishu-kyō]].

The name "Yonezawa" ({{nihongo|米澤|Yonezawa}}, literally "Rice Swamp") derives from this agricultural heritage, referring to the waterlogged paddies that produced abundant harvests. The [[Yonezawa Domain]], established during the [[Honda Shogunate]] (1601–1870), became one of the wealthiest fiefs in the realm due to its agricultural productivity and strategic position controlling inland trade routes.

===Industrialization and the Railway Crossroads===

[[File:Yonezawa Station 1900.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Yonezawa Station in 1900, shortly after becoming the railway crossroads of central Harujima.]]

The [[Saito Restoration]] (1871) transformed Yonezawa from an agricultural domain into an industrial powerhouse. The prefecture's central location made it the natural choice for railway junctions connecting the capital to the rest of Harujima. The [[Yonezawa Rail Junction]], completed in 1889, became the convergence point for lines running north, south, east, and west, establishing the prefecture as the '''logistics capital''' of the island.

This railway infrastructure attracted heavy industry. The [[Yonezawa Locomotive Works]], founded in 1892, began manufacturing steam engines and railway cars. By the early 20th century, the prefecture had developed into a major industrial center, with steel mills, textile factories, and machinery plants dotting the basin.

===Post-War Industrial Expansion===

Following the [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952), Yonezawa experienced explosive industrial growth. The prefecture's landlocked geography proved advantageous—safe from coastal bombing raids, the industrial facilities had survived the war largely intact. The post-war government designated Yonezawa as a "Priority Industrial Zone," attracting massive investment from [[zaibatsu]] conglomerates.

The [[Yonezawa Industrial Zone]], established in 1954, became the largest manufacturing complex in the Okami metropolitan region. Automotive manufacturers established assembly plants, electronics companies built component factories, and the logistics sector expanded to serve the growing capital market. The construction of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] through Yonezawa in 1967 further cemented the prefecture's role as the industrial heartland of the capital region.

===Modern Development===

The 1980s and 1990s saw Yonezawa diversify beyond manufacturing. Government administrative functions relocated from overcrowded Okami to satellite offices in Yonezawa, attracted by lower costs and modern facilities. Universities established campuses in the prefecture, creating an educational corridor. The [[Yonezawa Super Arena]], opened in 1998, became a major sports and entertainment venue serving the entire metropolitan region.

Today, Yonezawa maintains its dual identity as both a manufacturing powerhouse and a residential suburb for the capital. The prefecture's population has grown steadily as Okami's housing pressures push families inland, while the industrial sector continues to adapt to changing economic conditions.

==Geography==

Yonezawa Prefecture occupies a characteristic '''inland basin''' surrounded by hills on all sides. This landlocked geography creates a distinct microclimate and has historically protected the region from coastal typhoons and maritime invasions. The prefecture serves as the geographic center of Harujima island, making it the natural crossroads for transportation and trade.

===Topography===

The prefecture is dominated by the '''Yonezawa Basin''', a flat alluvial plain formed by ancient lakes and rivers. The basin is ringed by the '''Nishikawa Hills''' to the west, the '''Yamanobe Range''' to the south, and the '''Kahoku Highlands''' to the north. These elevated areas remain largely forested and provide watersheds for the basin's irrigation systems.

The highest point, [[Mount Iide]] (1,124 m), sits on the southern border and serves as a popular hiking destination. The [[Mogami River]] flows through the basin from west to east, providing water for both agriculture and industry. Numerous smaller tributaries have been channelized to support the extensive industrial water needs of the manufacturing zones.

The flat terrain of the basin has facilitated the development of extensive rail and road networks, making Yonezawa the most well-connected prefecture in terms of transportation infrastructure.

