Yonezawa Prefecture has a '''humid subtropical climate''' (Köppen ''Cfa'') with distinct seasonal variations. The landlocked basin geography creates greater temperature extremes than coastal prefectures—summers are hotter and winters cooler than the nearby [[Okami Metropolis]].

The surrounding hills create a partial '''rain shadow effect''', making Yonezawa drier than coastal prefectures. Annual precipitation averages 1,712mm, concentrated in the summer monsoon season (June–August). The basin is prone to '''heat island effects''' in summer, with temperatures frequently exceeding 35°C in July and August.

==Economy==

[[File:Yonezawa Automotive Assembly.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Yonezawa Motors assembly plant, the prefecture's largest private employer.]]

Yonezawa Prefecture's economy is defined by its role as the '''industrial engine''' of the capital region. The prefecture's GDP in 2025 was estimated at ε89.4 billion, making it one of the most productive prefectures in Okaiken on a per-capita basis. The economy is dominated by manufacturing, logistics, and government administration, with a growing service sector supporting the metropolitan population.

===Manufacturing===

Heavy manufacturing forms the backbone of Yonezawa's economy, with the [[Yonezawa Industrial Zone]] serving as the primary hub. The automotive sector represents the largest manufacturing segment, with [[Yonezawa Motors]] operating the prefecture's largest assembly plant, employing 18,000 workers and producing over 400,000 vehicles annually for the domestic market. [[Usami Heavy Industries]] manufactures industrial machinery, construction equipment, and railway rolling stock, while [[Nanyo Electronics]] produces consumer electronics, semiconductors, and precision components for the global market.

The [[Takahata Textile District]] continues the prefecture's historic textile tradition, now focused on technical fabrics and synthetic materials for automotive and industrial applications. The [[Shirataka Chemical Complex]] produces industrial chemicals, plastics, and pharmaceuticals for the capital region market.

===Logistics and Distribution===

Yonezawa's central location and extensive rail infrastructure have made it the '''logistics capital''' of Harujima island. The [[Yonezawa Logistics Hub]], covering over 400 hectares, contains warehouses, distribution centers, and intermodal transfer facilities serving the entire metropolitan region. Major retailers and manufacturers maintain regional distribution centers in Yonezawa, taking advantage of the prefecture's road and rail connections to reach all parts of the island within hours.

The [[Yonezawa Freight Terminal]] processes over 2 million shipping containers annually, making it the busiest inland freight facility in Okaiken. The [[Yonezawa Air Cargo Facility]], connected to the capital's airports by dedicated rail links, handles time-sensitive shipments for the high-tech manufacturing sector.

===Government and Administration===

As land values in [[Okami]] soared in the late 20th century, numerous government ministries and agencies relocated administrative functions to Yonezawa. The [[Yonezawa Government Complex]], established in 1982, houses regional offices of 14 national ministries, employing over 25,000 civil servants. The prefecture has become known as the "Second Capital" ({{nihongo|第二の首都|Daini no Shuto}}) due to the concentration of administrative functions.

===Commuter Economy===

Approximately 28% of Yonezawa's working population commutes daily to [[Okami]], primarily via the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] and conventional rail services. This commuter flow has driven extensive residential development, particularly in the northeastern cities nearest the metropolis. The [[Yonezawa Commuter Belt]] represents one of the largest suburban residential areas in the capital region.

===Agriculture===

Despite industrialization, agriculture remains significant in the southern and western portions of the prefecture. The [[Nagai Valley]] produces premium rice varieties, including the famous [[Yonezawa Premium]] brand sold in Okami's luxury markets. Vegetable farming serves the capital's fresh produce needs, while the [[Iide Highland]] produces specialty crops including mushrooms and mountain vegetables.

==Demographics==

Yonezawa Prefecture has a population of 2,090,036 (2026), distributed across 17 municipalities. The population has grown steadily as the prefecture has attracted both industrial workers and commuters seeking affordable housing near the capital. The prefecture has a relatively balanced age distribution, with significant populations of both working-age families and retirees attracted by lower costs of living.

