==Transportation==

[[File:Yonezawa Rail Junction.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Yonezawa Rail Junction, the busiest railway interchange in central Harujima.]]

===Rail===

Yonezawa Prefecture is the '''railway crossroads of Harujima''', with more rail lines converging in the prefecture than anywhere else on the island. The [[Harujima Shinkansen]] passes through Yonezawa, with stops at [[Yonezawa Station]], [[Usami Station]], and [[Sagae Station]]. Journey times from Yonezawa Station to [[Okami Central Station]] average 32 minutes on express services.

The [[Yonezawa Rail Junction]], the largest railway interchange in central Harujima, connects the Shinkansen main line with four conventional rail lines serving northern, southern, and western destinations. The junction processes over 500 trains daily, including commuter services, freight trains, and long-distance intercity services.

The [[Yonezawa Commuter Rail]] network, operated by [[Okaiken Railways]], provides frequent local service connecting the prefecture's cities to each other and to the capital. The [[Yonezawa Loop Line]] circles the basin, providing connectivity between industrial zones and residential areas.

===Roads===

The [[Yonezawa Expressway]] (Route 4) provides direct highway access to [[Okami]], with the journey taking approximately 35 minutes by car. The [[Central Harujima Expressway]] passes through Yonezawa, connecting the prefecture to destinations north and south. The [[Yonezawa Ring Road]] circles the basin, providing access to industrial zones and relieving congestion in the city center.

===Air===

While Yonezawa has no commercial airport, the [[Yonezawa Air Cargo Facility]] handles freight shipments. Most air travelers use [[Okami International Airport]], accessible via direct rail link in 45 minutes. The [[Yonezawa Heliport]] provides emergency medical and corporate transport services.

==Culture==

[[File:Yonezawa Super Arena.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Yonezawa Super Arena, the largest indoor entertainment venue in the capital region.]]

===Sports and Entertainment===

Yonezawa Prefecture is known as the "Sports Capital of the Capital Region" due to its extensive athletic facilities. The [[Yonezawa Super Arena]], opened in 1998, is the largest indoor entertainment venue in the Okami metropolitan region, hosting concerts, sporting events, and exhibitions with a capacity of 52,000. The arena has hosted the [[National Basketball Championship]] finals 12 times and is home to the [[Yonezawa Capitals]] professional basketball team.

The [[Yonezawa Prefectural Stadium]] hosts regional football matches and athletics competitions. The [[Nanyo Velodrome]] attracts cycling enthusiasts from across the region, while the [[Yonezawa Motorsports Complex]] hosts motorcycle and automobile racing events.

===Festivals===

The '''Yonezawa Industrial Festival''', held each October, celebrates the prefecture's manufacturing heritage with factory tours, machinery exhibitions, and industrial heritage displays. The '''Rice Harvest Festival''' in the [[Nagai Valley]] preserves traditional agricultural customs, including rice-planting dances and harvest ceremonies.

The '''Yonezawa Summer Festival''' features the distinctive '''Basin Bonfire Dance''' ({{nihongo|盆地篝火踊り|Bonchi Kabura Odori}}), where participants dance around massive bonfires lit in the basin center, visible from the surrounding hills.

===Cuisine===

{{See also|Sanese cuisine}}

Yonezawa cuisine reflects its agricultural heritage and industrial workforce culture. Notable dishes include '''Yonezawa Premium rice bowl''' ({{nihongo|米澤特上丼|Yonezawa Tokujō-don}}), featuring the prefecture's premium rice with local toppings; '''factory manju''' ({{nihongo|工場饅頭|Kōjō-manjū}}), sweet buns originally developed as energy snacks for industrial workers; and '''basin hot pot''' ({{nihongo|盆地鍋|Bonchi-nabe}}), a hearty stew traditionally prepared for factory shift workers.

==Education==

Yonezawa Prefecture has developed into a major educational center, with universities establishing campuses to serve the growing metropolitan population. The [[Yonezawa Institute of Technology]] is renowned for its engineering programs and maintains close ties with the prefecture's manufacturing sector. [[Yonezawa Metropolitan University]] offers business and public administration programs, while [[Nanyo Medical University]] trains healthcare professionals for the capital region. The prefecture hosts over 45,000 university students, contributing to a vibrant academic atmosphere.

==Government and politics==
{{Main|Politics of Yonezawa Prefecture}}

The [[Yonezawa Prefectural Government]] is headquartered in the [[Yonezawa Prefectural Office Building]], a 35-story tower that dominates the city center. The prefectural assembly consists of 72 members elected from single-member districts. The governor serves a four-year term and wields significant influence over the prefecture's industrial development policies.

Due to its large population of government employees and industrial workers, Yonezawa has traditionally been a stronghold of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]], though the [[Liberal Democratic Party]] has made gains in recent elections among small business owners and commuters.

